From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Playport authors <dev@playport.dev>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 04:12:28 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: wait again after unqueueing when a killed thread's
 wait returns on iOS

On iOS the server ends a thread killed in an in-process wait through
the wait itself (wine-unix, madeira-unix; PLA-229): NtWaitForAlertByThreadId
returns STATUS_THREAD_IS_TERMINATING once instead of ending it there,
because RtlWaitOnAddress's entry is on the thread's stack and still in
the futex queue, which every pseudo-process's x86-64 ntdll shares; a
thread ended with the entry queued made a parent fault unlinking it.
RtlWaitOnAddress takes the entry off the queue as usual and then calls
the wait again, which ends the thread. When a waker had already taken
the entry off, its wake was meant for this thread (a critical section's
hand-off) and is passed on with RtlWakeAddressSingle first: lost, it
left the section's other waiters parked for ever, and on iOS some
locks are shared by pseudo-processes (the childabort probe's parent
hung after a crashed child's thread died this way).

Class: upstream-bug
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-10-13-killed-threads-teardown.md
Offered-upstream: no; the shared futex queue and the iOS abort are the iOS port's
---
 dlls/ntdll/sync.c | 17 +++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+)

diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/sync.c b/dlls/ntdll/sync.c
index db1d74e..7c46c4b 100644
--- a/dlls/ntdll/sync.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/sync.c
@@ -1376,6 +1376,7 @@ NTSTATUS WINAPI RtlWaitOnAddress( const void *addr, const void *cmp, SIZE_T size
 {
     struct futex_queue *queue = get_futex_queue( addr );
     struct futex_entry entry;
+    BOOL queued = FALSE;
     NTSTATUS ret;
 
     TRACE("addr %p cmp %p size %#Ix timeout %s\n", addr, cmp, size, debugstr_timeout( timeout ));
@@ -1436,10 +1437,26 @@ NTSTATUS WINAPI RtlWaitOnAddress( const void *addr, const void *cmp, SIZE_T size
     {
         spin_lock( &queue->lock );
         if (entry.addr)
+        {
             list_remove( &entry.entry );
+            queued = TRUE;
+        }
         spin_unlock( &queue->lock );
     }
 
+    /* Playport (PLA-229): the server killed this thread while it waited, and the
+     * iOS wait returned this once instead of ending it there, with the entry on
+     * its stack still queued (on iOS the futex queue is shared by every
+     * pseudo-process, and so are some locks). A waker that had already taken the
+     * entry off meant its wake for this thread (a critical section's hand-off):
+     * pass it on to the next waiter, or that one waits for ever. Then wait again,
+     * now that the entry is gone: that call ends the thread. */
+    if (ret == STATUS_THREAD_IS_TERMINATING)
+    {
+        if (!queued) RtlWakeAddressSingle( addr );
+        NtWaitForAlertByThreadId( addr, timeout );
+    }
+
     TRACE("returning %#lx\n", ret);
 
     if (ret == STATUS_ALERTED) ret = STATUS_SUCCESS;
